<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.T9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="A2.T9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medical Datasets Opposite Energy-Based Pruning.</span>
This table presents student accuracy obtained by training on the highest <math id="A2.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\%</annotation></semantics></math> energy-score images of the reference sets. As explained in <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ A Dataset is Worth 1 MB" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, we unexpectedly observed higher student accuracy when applying the opposite filtering strategy on the medical datasets.
</span></figcaption>
